To make the browser URL show hawk.k8s.net, your UI app must run on a server behind that domain. Right now node app.js runs only on your laptop at:

http://localhost:5000
So the browser will show localhost.

Typical deployment flow:

Put doc-tracker-ui on the server/Kubernetes environment that serves hawk.k8s.net.
Run the Node UI there, usually on port 5000.
Configure ingress/reverse proxy so a path or host routes to the UI.
Example options:

http://hawk.k8s.net/ui
  -> doc-tracker-ui service on port 5000
or:

http://hawk-ui.k8s.net
  -> doc-tracker-ui service on port 5000
You’ll need something like this in Kubernetes:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: doc-tracker-ui
spec:
  selector:
    app: doc-tracker-ui
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 5000
And an ingress route:

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: doc-tracker-ui
spec:
  rules:
    - host: hawk.k8s.net
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /ui
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: doc-tracker-ui
                port:
                  number: 80
Then you’d open:

http://hawk.k8s.net/ui
One important caution: your API already seems to use hawk.k8s.net/api/.... If you also serve UI from the same host, use a separate path like /ui, or configure the root / carefully so it does not conflict with /api.

For local development, seeing localhost:5000 is normal and correct. For a hawk URL, the app must be deployed behind the hawk domain.